# Valgrind suppressions for ODBC DRIVER-INTERNAL defects.
#
# The valgrind tier in run_odbc.sh asserts that WE make no invalid access. A
# driver manager dlopens third-party libraries, and what those do inside
# themselves is not gBASIC's to fix or to fail on.
#
# Each entry below was ISOLATED before being suppressed -- reproduced by a
# plain C program that calls the ODBC API directly with string literals, with
# no gBASIC code in the stack at all. A suppression added without that step
# would be indistinguishable from hiding our own bug.
#
# The suppressions are NARROW on purpose: they name the specific driver object
# and entry point. A blanket "anything inside a driver .so" rule would also
# hide uninitialised data that WE passed in, which is precisely the defect
# class this tier exists to catch.
#
# THAT IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. tests/odbc_driver_probe.c is the
# isolation program, committed, and its --dirty mode hands SQLTables an
# unwritten stack buffer -- a caller's defect on purpose. A caller's
# uninitialised data is reported at strlen (vg_replace_strmem.c), valgrind's
# own interceptor OUTSIDE the driver, while the driver's own defect is reported
# at a frame inside it; so every entry below requires its INNERMOST frame to be
# the driver object, and none of them can match the former. run_odbc.sh asserts
# both directions on every run, and a blanket entry is proven red against the
# dirty probe.

# MariaDB Connector/ODBC 3.2.6 (libmaodbc.so) reads an uninitialised value in
# its own SQLPrepare path. Reproduced standalone with:
#   SQLPrepare(stmt, (SQLCHAR *)"select 1", SQL_NTS)
# on a literal, in C, outside gBASIC entirely.
{
   mariadb-odbc-sqlprepare-uninitialised
   Memcheck:Cond
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   fun:SQLPrepare
   fun:SQLPrepare
   ...
}

# THE FOUR CATALOG CALLS, added 2026-09-09 when `discovery` became the first
# thing to make them. The same unsymbolised function inside libmaodbc.so reads
# an uninitialised value on every one of them, reached from four entry points.
#
# Reproduced by tests/odbc_driver_probe.c -- committed this time, because the
# previous entry's isolation was done by hand and thrown away, which left the
# claim true when made and unverifiable afterwards. Every argument the probe
# passes is a string literal or SQL_NTS, so nothing of ours can be
# uninitialised, and the whole stack below the driver manager is `main`.
#
# THE INNERMOST FRAME IS REQUIRED TO BE INSIDE THE DRIVER, and that is what
# stops these entries hiding a CALLER that passes uninitialised data in.
# MEASURED, not argued: the probe's `--dirty` mode hands SQLTables an
# unwritten stack buffer, and valgrind reports that at `strlen
# (vg_replace_strmem.c)` -- its own interceptor, outside libmaodbc -- so an
# `at: obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so` anchor does not match it. run_odbc.sh asserts
# exactly that, both ways.

{
   mariadb-odbc-sqltables-uninitialised
   Memcheck:Cond
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   fun:SQLTables
   fun:SQLTables
   ...
}

{
   mariadb-odbc-sqlcolumns-uninitialised
   Memcheck:Cond
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   fun:SQLColumns
   fun:SQLColumns
   ...
}

{
   mariadb-odbc-sqlprimarykeys-uninitialised
   Memcheck:Cond
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   fun:SQLPrimaryKeys
   fun:SQLPrimaryKeys
   ...
}

{
   mariadb-odbc-sqlforeignkeys-uninitialised
   Memcheck:Cond
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   obj:*/odbc/libmaodbc.so
   fun:SQLForeignKeys
   fun:SQLForeignKeys
   ...
}

# NOT SUPPRESSED: the same driver defect is reachable through SQLExecDirect,
# which the probe once used to create its own fixture tables. gBASIC does not
# call SQLExecDirect -- it prepares and executes -- and the probe now takes the
# library's path too, so nothing here reproduces it any more. An entry the
# probe cannot produce is an entry with no evidence behind it, which is the one
# thing this file must not contain.
